Graded:   tester
Case:     AWPTCM-T43866 "(43) IPv4_MulticastRouting - VRF-Lite". No authored step text
          exists for this case. ck.db `zephyr_cases` id 40186, key AWPTCM-T43866,
          folder /New Platform Test (MASTER)/New Platform Template/IPv4, priority
          Normal, status Draft, carries NO objective, NO precondition and NO steps_text
          (`num_steps` 1 and an empty `steps` column), so there are no authored steps
          and no authored expected results. The headings below say what was actually
          exercised. The procedure was designed from:
            - the VRF-Lite Feature Overview and Configuration Guide C613-22142-00 REV B,
              whose central claim is "securely isolated" routing domains;
            - platforms/x960.md sections 7B.6-7B.7, the UNICAST VRF-Lite behaviours
              measured on this same DUT, so this case tests the MULTICAST path against a
              known unicast baseline;
            - the DUT's own CLI surface: `ip multicast-routing vrf`, `ip pim vrf`,
              `show ip mroute vrf`, `show ip igmp vrf`, `show ip pim vrf`;
            - finding F-9, a recorded inter-VRF UNICAST behaviour of this DUT, which this
              case deliberately re-tests on the multicast path (STEP 5).
          SCOPE, DECLARED: this case is DUT-LOCAL and uses ONE device. swi_b has no
          VRF-Lite licence at all (Base only; `VRF-LITE-FULL/63/8` absent), so it cannot
          be a VRF peer; swi_a holds the licence but was under a standing instruction
          never to be reloaded or power-cycled, and a second-router limb would add
          nothing this case needs. VRF-Lite isolation is a property of ONE router's
          forwarding tables, and the sharpest test of it is two segments on one router
          that can and cannot reach each other depending only on VRF membership. That is
          what is measured below, and it is why swi_d.cfg is the only config file.
          7 steps as executed, plus a baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
          AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone, console u2 @115200
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, captured 17:53 NZDT; saved to startup:
          no). DECLARED: this case's central A/B is moving `vlan48` between VRFs, so the
          configuration legitimately changes during the run. swi_d.cfg captures the
          BOTH-INTERFACES-IN-RED state, which is the one the persistence and removal
          steps ran on; the isolation state differs from it by exactly the three lines
          quoted in STEP 3.

VERDICT: PASS -- multicast routing on this build is genuinely per-VRF, and VRF-Lite
isolates the multicast path as it isolates the unicast one. The proof is counted traffic
between two host NICs on one router, with the capture proved live by the DUT's own
frames, and an A/B whose only variable is one interface's VRF membership.
