  => STEP 4 PASS: the per-VRF tables hold exactly what the dataplane did -- the joined
     group with `vlan48 (1)` in its outgoing interface list and the never-joined control
     group with an empty one, in the same table at the same instant. The read-back worth
     knowing about is recorded as O-29: `show ip mroute` with NO `vrf` keyword prints
     the VRF sections too, headed `[VRF: red]`, and its output here was identical to
     `show ip mroute vrf red`. The global table was empty, because global multicast
     routing is off, so a reader skimming `show ip mroute` would reasonably but wrongly
     conclude these entries are in the global table. Read the `[VRF: ...]` heading.

STEP 5 -- the F-9 check: does a stale entry keep forwarding after the VRF changes?  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: F-9 is a recorded behaviour of this DUT on the UNICAST path -- removing an
  inter-VRF static route left the dataplane forwarding across the VRF boundary from a
  stale ARP-derived per-host entry until `clear arp-cache vrf` was run, configuration
  and route table both saying closed while traffic flowed. The multicast path was tested
  for the same shape in the sharpest form available: with (10.38.22.40, 239.30.30.30)
  ACTIVELY forwarding and its outgoing interface list holding `vlan48 (1)`, vlan48 was
  moved straight back into blue and the leg re-run IMMEDIATELY, with no re-join and no
  `clear` of anything.

awplus(config-if)#no ip vrf forwarding red
awplus(config-if)#ip vrf forwarding blue
awplus(config-if)#ip address 10.38.22.66/27

cap v5-f9-check-eth4.pcap: 0 frame(s)
awplus#show ip mroute vrf red
(10.38.22.40, 239.30.30.30), uptime 00:03:18   Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
  Incoming interface: vlan10
  Outgoing interface list:              <-- EMPTIED immediately
(10.38.22.40, 239.31.31.31) ...
  Outgoing interface list:

  => STEP 5 PASS, and F-9's shape does NOT occur in the multicast path. The outgoing
     interface list emptied the moment the interface left the VRF and the dataplane
     stopped in the same instant -- no stale forwarding and nothing to clear -- and the
     40 s re-run quoted in STEP 2 confirms it with two of the DUT's own PIM hellos and
     zero subject frames. This is a useful negative: the multicast outgoing interface is
     torn down with the interface, whereas the unicast hazard of F-9 lives in an
     ARP-derived host entry that outlives its route.

